\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The witness values occupy these double cosets}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{For the pairing kernel of \ref{fdip-012F} on the vector of
\ref{fdip-0126}, the value #{3} occupies one double coset of size
#{3}, and the value #{1} occupies one double coset of size #{3}.
The value #{2} occupies two double cosets of size #{9}. Each of
the values #{5/2} and #{3/2} occupies two double cosets of size
#{3} and two of size #{9}.}

\proof{
\p{By \ref{fdip-012F} and \ref{fdip-012O}, the values of #{B} are
#{3}, #{5/2}, #{2}, #{3/2} and #{1}, on #{3}, #{24}, #{18},
#{24} and #{3} elements. The value #{3} occurs exactly on the
stabilizer, which is one double coset of size #{3}. The value
#{1} occupies #{3} elements, hence one further double coset of
size #{3}. \ref{fdip-0126} supplies #{6} double cosets of size
#{3} and #{6} of size #{9}, so #{4} of size #{3} and #{6} of size
#{9} remain for the three middle values. A double coset carries
one value. The count #{18} uses cosets of these two sizes.
#{6} cosets of size #{3} would be required to make #{18} with no
size #{9}, and only #{4} remain. Three or more cosets of size
#{9} exceed #{18}. Two combinations remain. Two cosets of size
#{9} make #{18}. One coset of size #{9} leaves #{9}, hence three
cosets of size #{3}. That split leaves one coset of size #{3}
and #{5} of size #{9}. From those remaining sizes, no combination
sums to #{24}: three or more cosets of size #{9} exceed #{24};
two sum to #{18}, and the one remaining coset of size #{3} brings
the total to #{21}; one leaves #{15}, which would require #{5}
cosets of size #{3}, and only one remains; none leaves only that
one coset, whose size is #{3} rather than #{24}. Thus the count #{18} uses two cosets of
size #{9}. That choice leaves #{4} cosets of each size for the
two blocks of #{24}. A block of #{24} cannot use three cosets of
size #{9}, since #{27} already exceeds #{24}. It cannot use one:
the remainder #{15} would require #{5} cosets of size #{3}, and
only #{4} remain. It cannot use none: that would require #{8}
cosets of size #{3}, and only #{4} remain. Thus each block uses
two cosets of size #{9} and two of size #{3}.}
}
